{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1774358603877,"sessionID":"ses_2dffee6f6ffeAowAs1Fn0JzBmI","part":{"id":"prt_d200136c3001IpvT486ZJVn7AQ","sessionID":"ses_2dffee6f6ffeAowAs1Fn0JzBmI","messageID":"msg_d20013150001QIKwvl1DFULhj7","type":"tool","callID":"tooluse_94pkK9PoI5tVPpahxjpdoN","tool":"task","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"subagent_type":"explore","description":"Explore assertion rewriter code","prompt":"Look at the assertion rewriter code in /tmp/swebench-workspaces/pytest-dev__pytest-5103/src/_pytest/assertion/. Find where `all()` and `any()` builtin functions are currently being handled, or if they're not handled at all. Also look at test files to understand what changes are expected. Return:\n1. How `all()` and `any()` are currently handled in the rewriter\n2. What needs to be changed to unroll these iterables for better error messages\n3. Any existing tests related to all/any"},"output":"task_id: ses_2dffec31dffejuWyHikxDtrXT9 (for resuming to continue this task if needed)\n\n<task_result>\nPerfect! Now let me create one final comprehensive summary:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've completed a thorough analysis of the assertion rewriter code in pytest. Here are my findings:\n\n### **1. How `all()` and `any()` Are Currently Handled**\n\n**Current Status**: `all()` and `any()` are **NOT specially handled** in the current codebase (HEAD commit 443dffc8).\n\n**Location**: `/private/tmp/swebench-workspaces/pytest-dev__pytest-5103/src/_pytest/assertion/rewrite.py` (lines 967-1036)\n\n**Current Behavior**:\n- The `visit_Call_35()` and `visit_Call_legacy()` methods treat `all()` and `any()` like any other function call\n- They wrap the result in variable assignments (`@py_assert0`, `@py_assert1`, etc.)\n- Only show True/False result with no information about which element failed\n\n**Example of current error message**:\n```\nassert all(x == 1 for x in range(10))\nAssertionError: assert False\n```\n(No indication of which x value failed)\n\n---\n\n### **2. What Needs to Be Changed**\n\nThe feature needs to **unroll `all()` calls** to show which specific element caused the failure.\n\n**Implementation Strategy**:\n1. **Detect**: Check if Call node is `all()` with exactly one argument that's a GeneratorExp or ListComp\n2. **Extract**: Get the loop variable, iterable, and test condition\n3. **Rewrite**: Create an equivalent assertion that unrolls the generator/comprehension\n4. **Report**: Show the failing element with detailed information\n\n**Code Locations to Modify**:\n- `/private/tmp/swebench-workspaces/pytest-dev__pytest-5103/src/_pytest/assertion/rewrite.py`\n  - Add `_is_any_call_with_generator_or_list_comprehension()` method (before line 967)\n  - Add `_visit_all()` method (around line 990)\n  - Modify `visit_Call_35()` (line 967) to check for all() calls\n  - Modify `visit_Call_legacy()` (line 1000) to check for all() calls\n\n**Expected output after implementation**:\n```\nassert False\nwhere False = check_even(1)  # showing which element and why it failed\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. Existing Tests Related to all/any**\n\n**Test File**: `/private/tmp/swebench-workspaces/pytest-dev__pytest-5103/testing/test_assertrewrite.py`\n\nFour tests have been added (lines 659-725) that define the expected behavior:\n\n1. **`test_unroll_expression`** (659-663): Expects \"0 == 1\" in error message\n2. **`test_unroll_generator`** (680-693): Expects \"assert False\" and \"where False = check_even(1)\"\n3. **`test_unroll_list_comprehension`** (695-708): Same as above but with list comprehension\n4. **`test_for_loop`** (710-725): Reference test showing desired output format\n\nThe tests show that the feature should unroll `all()` calls to produce detailed failure messages similar to writing individual assertions in a loop.\n\n---\n\n### **Bonus: Git History**\n\nThe feature was actually implemented before but then reverted:\n- **a0dbf2ab** (Apr 22, 2019): Added test cases\n- **22d91a3c** (May 18, 2019): Initial implementation\n- **acdf30a5** (Jun 2, 2019): Bug fixes for extended cases\n- **1b381d52**: Feature was REVERTED\n\nThis is why the tests exist but the implementation is not present!\n</task_result>","title":"Explore assertion rewriter code","metadata":{"sessionId":"ses_2dffec31dffejuWyHikxDtrXT9","model":{"modelID":"anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0","providerID":"amazon-bedrock"},"truncated":false},"time":{"start":1774358445281,"end":1774358603876}}}}
